WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.315
Six Moves, from EL Publishing. Move two: sentence frames in four lanes.

2
00:00:05.765 --> 00:00:11.127
You ask the class a good question: why is this a plant cell and not an animal cell? Hands go up.

3
00:00:11.127 --> 00:00:16.824
The same four hands. Your English learners know the answer. They don't have the sentence to put it in.

4
00:00:17.274 --> 00:00:21.793
A sentence frame is the sentence with the content missing. This is a blank cell.

5
00:00:21.793 --> 00:00:25.972
The frame carries the grammar, so the student only has to supply the idea.

6
00:00:25.972 --> 00:00:28.627
A level-one student says: this is a plant cell.

7
00:00:28.627 --> 00:00:33.711
That's a correct spoken answer in academic English, from a student who arrived last month.

8
00:00:34.161 --> 00:00:39.610
Here's the move. Don't post one frame. Post four, one per lane, and let students pick.

9
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:44.326
Lane one, for a level one or two: one blank, and a word bank next to it.

10
00:00:44.776 --> 00:00:49.431
Lane two: a frame with a reason built in. It is a plant cell because it has a blank.

11
00:00:49.881 --> 00:00:55.003
Lane three, for a level three: I think blank because blank. Now they're building the sentence.

12
00:00:55.453 --> 00:01:00.883
Lane four, for a level four and for your native speakers: although blank, the evidence shows blank.

13
00:01:00.883 --> 00:01:04.393
They would not say that on their own. With the frame, they will.

14
00:01:04.843 --> 00:01:07.714
Students usually pick the right lane for themselves.

15
00:01:07.714 --> 00:01:11.027
When someone picks too low, you nudge: try lane three today.

16
00:01:11.477 --> 00:01:16.224
Running it takes seven or eight minutes. The question and the four frames go on the board.

17
00:01:16.224 --> 00:01:18.070
Thirty seconds of silence to think.

18
00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:22.870
Partner A talks for a minute; partner B listens and says back one thing they heard. Switch.

19
00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:28.355
Then you pick two or three students to share with the class — you pick, so it isn't the same four hands.

20
00:01:28.805 --> 00:01:30.480
The say-it-back step matters.

21
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:36.371
If a level-one student says a word and their partner has to repeat it, the word was heard. It counted.

22
00:01:36.371 --> 00:01:37.468
Nobody got ignored.

23
00:01:37.918 --> 00:01:43.929
Without frames, you get four hands, a silent newcomer, and one-word answers from the students who could do more.

24
00:01:43.929 --> 00:01:52.301
With four lanes, everyone speaks academic English twice, the newcomer says a whole sentence, and your level fours are using although and the evidence shows.

25
00:01:52.751 --> 00:01:54.979
Tomorrow: one question, four frames.

26
00:01:54.979 --> 00:02:00.674
There are a hundred and fifty of them sorted by level in the Sentence-Frame Bank on the hub.

27
00:02:00.674 --> 00:02:04.017
Free, at radan55 dot github dot io slash everyteacher.
